e  I said it's getting back outside it's  late in the evening this is he has a  flashlight and he made me go out and  look for that pencil for nearly two  hours and bring it to him and then he  made me sit next to him and he said now  I'm going to explain why I did this and  said I want you to learn two lessons  from this one is that even in the making  of a little pencil not a lot of natural  resources of the world are used and if  all of us millions of us go around  wasting pencils like this imagine how  many how much of natural resources be  erasing and we have no right to do this  now it amazes me that he was thinking  about environment and ecology in 1945-46  when that wasn't even an issue I don't  think even anybody knew the word  environment and ecology but that was one  lesson that he wanted me to learn he  says however small or however  insignificant it may seem to you never  get into the habit of wasting things  destroying things use it to the ultimate  the other lesson was that there were  millions of children around the world  who lived in such abject poverty that  they didn't have even these little  things to use so we ought to be doubly  careful about all the things that we get  and we are privileged to have so that if  we don't waste we would be able to share  with many more people what kinds of  things that your grandfather like to do  for fun I like the games you would play  what type things  can laugh for instance oh you would  laugh at many things he had this great  sense of humor and I remember when we  used to go out for walks in the morning  and evening and onion he always had the  habit of keeping two people close to him  and he would lean on their shoulders  keep his arms on their shoulders and  walked it and very often just while  walking you know it suddenly lifts up  his legs and angles okay if you're not  not aware of this and you're taken by  surprise you could collapse on it  whenever he would play such games  sometimes he had a walking