ade it illegal to discriminate  against somebody on the basis of caste  or religion or race of or gender the low  caste people who were discriminated  against they had to go and prove to the  the police and the courts of law that  they have been oppressed or  discriminated and these people didn't  have the means they didn't have the  education they didn't have the finances  to do these things and also very often  the person who oppressed them or  discriminated against them was their  employer so they couldn't very well go  and Institute legal proceedings against  somebody who's providing them bread and  butter  you can also probably have to go up  before a judge who was at a higher caste  yeah right yeah all of these things put  together they never did institute any  cases and so you go to India today and  government will give you statistics to  show that there are no cases of  discrimination in India Africa there  were that's only half the truth who has  nobody has instituted the proceedings  how can there be any statistics of cases  then at all did  Nehru try to bust the caste system were  there any progressive social changes  under his rule he was one of the  lieutenant's of your grandfather who was  taken to be one of the more progressive  of the Indian socialist national and  Indian nationalists for independence and  was quite a distinguished person ruled  India for a long time did he not make an  effort to follow your grandfather's  teaching in terms of eliminating the  caste system or was he a disappointment  well I personally was very disappointed  with him I thought that he would do much  more in this field to break the caste  system there but obviously he didn't do  it and I think the only reason I can  find it is that at the time of  Independence  more than 99.9 percent of the government  and the bureaucracy was made up of the  two upper caste people the Brahmins and  exoti as niro himself was a Brahmin and  so they didn't want to threaten their  own existence and and so the only thing  that I ended up doing is like I said and  putting it in the Constitution that they  will have equal rights and they would  not be discriminated just as we did with  the blacks here in the United States  they were granted civil rights but that  was all not legally you can do all these  things but then to implement that in in  real life it's very difficult we see  that in the United States also and we  see it in India that we are not able to  translate the law in  in everyday life there so the actual  integration of the people hasn't taken  place now Indira Gandhi for many years  was also the Prime Minister of the ruler  president of India what's the  relationship between your family and  Indira Gandhi's family and what is your  sense of her to the extent that she did  or did not follow the legacy of your  grandfather well first of all let me say  that she wasn't related to us in any way  at all in fact she was very astute in  misleading people not only in India but  outside about this relationship and let  me explain this the Gandhi that she  married is was a descendant from Iran  known as a Parsi and they used to spell  their name they did have the family name  Gandhi but they used to spell it very  differently it was G H a and D H hy and  she changed it to the Hindu spelling of  the name to confuse people and see guys  she got that you know Sid she was able  to do that and so a lot of people  everywhere in the world today think that  she was directly related to the Gandhi  so to that extent he used Gandhi's name  there but unfortunately she didn't do  anything concrete or progressive to  implement his philosophy I don't think  she really believed in anything very  much she's just a politician he was a  politician she was out for power and she  was one of those people who believed  that Gandhi  wasn't realistic his ideas of cottage  industry and all that were outdated and  they were not the answer to India but  that was because they 