perience and then they  began to sell that milk to the  government and that became an income for  them and from that we help them develop  an agricultural cooperative and through  these cooperatives in about eight or  nine years we transform the economic  base of that village totally they began  to get steady income and sizeable income  every month  and they began to eat well and you know  wear clothes and send their children to  schools and all those things and and you  could see that transformation in them  and then we induce them to save some  money for various programs of their own  like medical facilities and scholarships  and things like that and then there was  another big problem there in that in  that whole area because of poverty there  the agencies to come from the cities and  by young girls to take them away into  prostitution and they would buy these  girls for $12 and $15 and they were gone  you know at the age of 1012 there and  they went and they never came back again  because they told the parents that they  were taking them to the city and going  to give them jobs and all that in  somebody's homes but in actual fact they  were taken to prostitution so we wanted  to put an end to that and we told these  people like now you must ensure that  nobody reaches that stage of desperation  that they have to sell their daughters  into prostitution and you set aside some  money for that and they didn't and they  also created a volunteer group of young  men who would scour around the villages  in that area and see that nobody ever  sold their daughters and whenever  anybody was in a desperate need of money  or something they would help them  through this fun so since 1973 and they  stopped the sale of pregnant girls in  about nearly 200 villages in the name of  her  remar and and not only that but then you  know we had inspired them all along that  they should not become selfish that once  they have a 10 deck anomic stability  they should be willing to help ot