a the present  Prime Minister asking him to donate  through the Government of India  all the books written by a known Gandhi  the videotapes and the films and audio  tapes and all the things that they have  there please make a copy of it and give  it to me so that I can keep it in this  Institute and it will help in teaching  young people the philosophy of  non-violence and I got a letter saying  that he was sending it over to the  relevant ministries and I will hear from  them shortly well I did hear from them a  year later and they said we have passed  all your requests you can take all these  things but we don't have the money to  mail it to you to ship it to you so you  will have to make arrangements to ship  it you will have to make arrangements  for somebody to copy all the films and  the photographs and all that pay for the  copies and so I said okay that's fine  find some donor in the US who can do it  and then two months later I read in the  newspapers the government of india  started a narrow center in london and  they sent spent three million dollars  there to renovate the building and to  you know make spruce it up and there's  now shipping all the furniture required  for that center from india at the  expense of the government your  grandfather's philosophy represents a  threat to the present day rulers of  India as well as other oppressive  governments all over the world and I  just do not want to support social  movements and social activism that  threatens the status quo whereas Nehru  is a Western Iser a moderate politician  as he turns out to be who's very Pro  British in certain ways oh yeah as  evidenced by the fact that in London  they're having this Center so it's not  terribly surprising let's conclude the  discussion we're talking about how you  came to the United States you were  involved in the struggles in India that  you told us in detail about to help the  villagers and particularly the  untouchable class and fight the caste  system what led you to leave India and  to come to the United States well I had  all these experiences in India and in  South Africa and especially in India  where I used Graham grandfather's  philosophy very effectively in bringing  about these social and economic changes  there and I thought that that was  something that I needed to share with  other people and there was an American  friend was visiting India this was in 82  83 and I just got talking to him about  this work and he came and saw the work  that we were doing and  I was very impressed by and while we  were talking the idea came into my mind  why not write a book comparing these  attitudes in South Africa India and the  United States one place you have the  Glacial discrimination in another place  to catch discrimination and in the third  place the color discrimination compare  this and find nonviolent solutions to it  and write a book on heaven he was very  taken up by the idea and he said if  you'd like to come to the US and do that  I could make it possible for you so he  arranged for a fellowship for one year  to come get and do it and that's how I  came here in 87 and while working on  that project in Mississippi the media  heard about me and and I started getting  invited by universities to speak and all  that and so after the fellowship ended  we just extended our stay with this  money that I was earning through  speaking engagements and at the same  time working on the e-book and one thing  led to another and then in 88 my mother  died and that's when I went to South  Africa to be by her side and it was the  first time I saw the Gandhi Institute in  South Africa totally destroyed and  demolished it was a double tragedy for  me it was on one hand I lost my mother  and I see their life's worked with all  the Nashes you know somebody burned it  down yeah it was the government and the  cutter together  you know conspired to demolish it so I  was thinking of starting an Institute  for the Study of non-violence initially  the idea was to do it in South Africa  sort of replacement of this Institute  that was destroyed and just to you know  inform young people of alternative ways  of dealing with conflicts there but  South Africa wasn't ready for it they  didn't accept the idea I and a lot of  political hassles is in 88 so I gave up  the idea and lots of my friends here in  the academic world they said if you  can't do it in South Africa why don't  you do it here it's needed anywhere in  the world so I looked around here and  the Christian Brothers University in  Memphis a very small University heard  about it so they offered us some  hospitality and said that they didn't  have the money but they could we could  use the facilities and I thought that  was good enough so I sold my  grandfather's letters that I inherited  from my mother the original letters a  packet of about 67 letters they were all  right from 1892 to 1948 and we were  getting old and brittle and  you know deteriorating very quickly and  I thought that I wouldn't be able to  preserve them so if I could raise some  money from that why not do that and I  did that last year I optioned them in  London but that auction caused a little  controversy in India the Government of  India accused me of commercializing God  his name and all that so the price of  the letters fell and when I got fifty  six thousand dollars and that was  invested in this Institute which is what  we are working with now but now a lot of  people locally have heard about it that  they are very sympathetic and they give  us donations so we have been working for  the last one year in that Institute and  I think we'll be able to do quite a lot  of work there one of the things that we  want to take up immediately is 1994 is  the grandfather 125th birth anniversary  and I wanted to use that as a peg to  declare 1994 as a year of non-violence  to commemorate all the people all over  the world who sacrificed for the  nonviolent change you know and and  simultaneously have programs to you know  inform people and they educate people in  nonviolent conflict resolution it's  ironic that his home country has turned  his back on him but the rest of the  world has really embraced him and his  philosophy and that's what I was saying  that the rest of the world is it  respects him and how is this have you  seen this happening in various different  parts of the world the impact well I  think it's been tremendous and and  wherever I've been outside India the  interest in Gandhi and the in his  philosophy and all that has been  tremendous and we can see it in everyday  life I mean the influences in Eastern  Europe and all over the place changes  have been taking place in a non-violent  manner and much of that has been his  influence one way or the other  and do you envisage participated in some  nonviolent social change and activism  projects in the United States as you all  know or in Luther King in the civil  rights movement was deeply influenced by  your grandfather's teaching disease and  contemporary projects that this might be  applied in the United States one of the  things that I want to do very soon is to  hold a conference of youth here we see  the youths having many problems that is  violence among the youth and and a lot  of disaffection among the youths and  drugs and all that now all of these  things tell me that there is a lot of  frustration among the youth but we have  never consulted the youth about it it's  always the experts the adults who  analyze the problems of the youth and  provide the solutions and those  solutions are not accepted by the youth  so you know things just get worse and  worse what I want to do is to reverse  the process they have this conference of  the youth where the youths are going to  talk they are going to help find try to  find some solutions we can help them in  whatever way they want to but they must  take the initiative and they must come  up with the solutions there and we  adults must sit and listen to them I  think that's what we need to do in many  things in fact this is what I've been  trying to  explained to many of the civil rights  leaders and also people in Memphis that  we now have to change our focus from  protesting to doing something positive  you're in for long we have been  protesting and protesting and protesting  let's get down to doing something  positive ourselves and not wait for the  government to do things
 

